# Chavruta.AI FULL-CORPUS retrieval eval v1 — the gate for `chavruta_commercial` (Principle V).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   eval/tanakh_v1.jsonl is historical: it was written for a Tanakh-only index, and several of its
#   "unanswerable" items (Mishnah, Shulchan Aruch, Gemara) are now IN the corpus, so a low score there
#   measures the dataset, not the system. This set covers the 15 tiers actually loaded, across every
#   intent the product ships.
#
# FORMAT  {"qid","question","lang","expected_refs":[...],"intent","note"}
#   expected_refs are refs in the corpus's own scheme — Sefaria underscore-dot ('Genesis.1.1',
#   'Bava_Metzia.3.1'), Talmud amud-linear (N = 2·daf−1 for amud a, so Berakhot 2a → Berakhot.3.1).
#   The harness matches SEGMENT-WISE, so a shorter ref is a legitimate looser expectation:
#   'Rashi_on_Genesis.1.1' matches 'Rashi_on_Genesis.1.1.3', and 'Genesis.1' matches 'Genesis.1.3'.
#   expected_refs == [] ⇒ the question names a work the corpus does NOT hold and an honest no-source
#   answer is the pass (SC-002).
#
# HOW THE REFS WERE CHOSEN
#   From the sugya, then verified to exist in the live collection — never from what retrieval
#   happened to return. An eval assembled out of the retriever's own output measures nothing, and an
#   eval expecting a source the index does not hold measures less than that. Every non-empty ref
#   below was confirmed present in chavruta_commercial on 2026-07-27.
#
# THE UNANSWERABLE ITEMS ARE STABLE BY LICENCE, NOT BY ACCIDENT
#   They name 20th-century works (Rav Kook, Soloveitchik, Igrot Moshe, Shemirat Shabbat KeHilchata,
#   Tzitz Eliezer). The corpus is 100% commercially-licensed — PD/CC0/CC-BY/CC-BY-SA only — so those
#   cannot enter it without a licence change, and the item stays valid as the corpus grows.
#
# Run:  python scripts/run_eval.py --retrieval-only --dataset eval/corpus_v1.jsonl
#
